WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.140
In the first film, whatever could cross ran downhill for free.

2
00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:13.020
So how does a cell keep so much sodium outside, and so much potassium inside?

3
00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:16.153
Look at an animal cell's membrane.

4
00:00:17.973 --> 00:00:20.813
Sodium shown in orange, is much higher outside the cell.

5
00:00:22.213 --> 00:00:24.893
Potassium shown in purple, is much higher inside.

6
00:00:26.313 --> 00:00:28.333
Each ion sits at the top of its own hill.

7
00:00:29.367 --> 00:00:31.627
A few channels in the membrane leak.

8
00:00:33.307 --> 00:00:35.807
Sodium trickles in, and potassium trickles out.

9
00:00:37.087 --> 00:00:40.507
Each ion runs downhill, and the two differences start to shrink.

10
00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:46.030
A leak channel is an open tunnel, so ions pass through it only downhill.

11
00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:50.830
For ions, the hill also depends on the membrane's voltage.

12
00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:54.090
Biology II covers that part.

13
00:00:55.330 --> 00:00:56.330
A pump is different.

14
00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:01.730
It grabs ions, changes shape, and spends energy to carry them uphill.

15
00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.880
Watch one cycle of the sodium-potassium pump.

16
00:01:07.080 --> 00:01:11.160
First, three sodium ions bind to the pump on the inside of the cell.

17
00:01:12.283 --> 00:01:18.043
The pump spends one ATP, the molecule that carries usable energy around the cell.

18
00:01:19.243 --> 00:01:23.163
The pump changes shape and releases the three sodium ions outside.

19
00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:27.117
Now two potassium ions bind on the outside.

20
00:01:28.377 --> 00:01:30.997
The pump changes shape again and carries them in.

21
00:01:32.257 --> 00:01:35.057
Both trips went uphill, against each ion's gradient.

22
00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:41.030
Every cycle, one ATP spent, three sodium out, two potassium in.

23
00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:46.450
While the pump keeps running, the meters hold steady, even though the leaks never

24
00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:47.450
close.

25
00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:53.410
Movement that uses the cell's energy, often from ATP, is active transport.

26
00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:56.950
It can move a substance against its gradient.

27
00:01:58.390 --> 00:02:03.090
Diffusion and osmosis are passive transport, and need no energy from the cell.

28
00:02:04.133 --> 00:02:06.033
Now the cell runs out of ATP.

29
00:02:07.333 --> 00:02:08.733
The leak channels stay open.

30
00:02:10.073 --> 00:02:11.593
Which way will sodium move now?

31
00:02:12.753 --> 00:02:14.573
And which way will potassium move?

32
00:02:15.893 --> 00:02:16.893
Pause and predict.

33
00:02:23.367 --> 00:02:29.367
The pump freezes. Sodium leaks in and potassium leaks out, both downhill.

34
00:02:30.707 --> 00:02:32.367
The two differences slowly shrink.

35
00:02:33.607 --> 00:02:35.987
The first film's rule has taken over again.

36
00:02:37.017 --> 00:02:39.997
So holding a difference is not a one-time payment.

37
00:02:41.197 --> 00:02:43.457
The leaks undo the pump's work all the time.

38
00:02:44.657 --> 00:02:48.057
The pump must keep spending ATP, cycle after cycle.

39
00:02:49.337 --> 00:02:51.577
Stop paying, and the differences drain away.

40
00:02:52.650 --> 00:02:56.190
That gives you one test for a substance crossing on its own.

41
00:02:57.470 --> 00:02:58.470
Is it going downhill?

42
00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:01.110
Then it is passive and free.

43
00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:03.330
Is it going uphill?

44
00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:05.610
Then the cell is paying.
